Subject: [PATCH 1/3] =?UTF-8?q?threads:=20a=20message=20that=20has=20a=20t?=
 =?UTF-8?q?hread=20is=20answered=20=E2=80=94=20tie=20at=20ingest=20from=20?=
 =?UTF-8?q?the=20parent's=20own=20thread=20summary,=20and=20let=20any=20fo?=
 =?UTF-8?q?reign=20in-thread=20reply=20answer=20(threaded=5Fis=5Fanswered,?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20default=20on)?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Measured on the aws-do-agent integration (operator, 2026-10-31): asks whose
thread anyone could see on the platform read UNANSWERED on the dashboard, and
kept being listed so. Two causes in the engine. The thread sweep is the only
route to answered for an in-thread reply, and it is bounded (SWEEP_MAX roots
per adapter per cycle, a 429 ends it), so a backfilled channel's answered asks
stay open for as long as the window takes to reach them. And in the sweep a
reply that classifies as an ask of its own is refused as the answer (review
F7), so an agent's reply that ends in a question leaves its ask open although
the thread visibly has an answer under it.

The change, behind one per-instance setting, threaded_is_answered (default
true; false restores today's behaviour exactly):

* channels/CONTRACT.md: the optional capability thread_summary(message) ->
  {reply_count, repliers, latest_reply} | None — what one polled row says about
  the thread under it. The slack adapter reads it back from the raw row
  (conversations.history serves a thread parent with reply_count, reply_users
  and latest_reply, and the first poll of a channel serves every parent that
  way); the fake adapter derives it from the messages it holds.
* core/threads.py answered_from_summary(): for each polled top-level message
  whose journal row is an open ask, the first replier other than the asker in
  the summary ties the ask (key thread-reply:summary:<replier>, so the
  dashboard's answered-by-thread count includes it), with no platform call. The
  asker alone stays open; the unattributed placeholder answers nothing; a count
  with no named replier stays open (a guess, and the engine never guesses); an
  adapter without the capability yields None (declared absent, never "no
  thread"). The tie is stamped at the sighting: the reply's own platform time
  predates the row's first sighting and would read as EDITED AFTER RESPONSE.
* core/schedule.py: the cycle ties after journaling and before the cursor
  commits (a crash between the two re-polls and ties again), and with the
  setting on passes the sweep no is_answer verdict — a foreign reply answers
  whatever it says, and a follow-up question is still owed on its own row. The
  sweep stays the route for a thread that grows after its parent was polled: a
  cursored history poll never serves a parent twice.
* core/config.py + scripts/scheduler.py: instances[].threaded_is_answered, a
  JSON boolean (a quoted "false" is refused, like escalate_ambiguous), carried
  on the Source.

Docs: QUICKSTART (three routes to answered; the top-level-reply limit kept),
README's slack row, core/README's threads row, the contract table, the slack
adapter's README; tests/test_docs.py pins the new wording.

Tests: tests/test_threads.py (the summary tie: a foreign replier ties with the
summary key and no replies() call; the first foreign replier is named; the
asker alone, an unattributed replier and a count without repliers stay open;
only open asks are tied; a reply row ties nothing; an absent capability reads
None; the stamp keeps edited_after_response empty; the fake's summary),
tests/test_slack_adapter.py (the summary read from a history parent's raw row;
None for a reply row and a foreign row), tests/test_schedule.py (a backfilled
channel of 2*SWEEP_MAX answered asks closes in one cycle with zero replies()
calls; by default a follow-up question answers the ask it is under and stays
owed itself; the F7 behaviour under the setting off; the tie counts as a thread
answer on the dashboard and closes the owed work), tests/test_portability.py
(the key: default on, read from the instance, a quoted value refused).
Red first: on the code with the setting default and the old F7 cycle test,
that test failed (a question under the ask now answers it); the new tests fail
on main (answered_from_summary and thread_summary do not exist there).
python3 -m unittest discover -s tests: Ran 873 tests, one failure that is the
same on main in this sandbox (test_portability's unreadable-subdirectory case,
a host permission check). tests/mutation_check.sh: 12 new mutations, two
re-anchored (the journal/cursor order mutation now spans the tie block; the
"sweeps without the classifier's verdict" mutation pins the setting-off path),
each caught.

