@@ -36,6 +36,7 @@ demands**: a missing optional method changes what the engine can prove, not whet
 | Method | Why, and what is lost without it |
 |---|---|
 | `retrievable_ts(channel, oldest?, latest?) -> set[str]` | Every timestamp the platform will serve **right now** — the third opinion `core/parity.py` needs. The incumbent oracle is an *archive*, not ground truth: it holds messages the platform has since deleted, and the engine, which can only fetch what the API serves, then gets blamed for "losing" them. Measured 2026-08-26 on the first live R8 window: 342 of 507 oracle rows in one channel were no longer served (one app's deleted burst), while the engine held 100% of retrievable history. Without this method the differ is **fail-closed** — every miss counts as a loss — so parity can never go green on a channel with any deleted history. The snapshot must be as gap-free as `poll()`: a truncated snapshot marks still-served messages "deleted upstream", which is the one direction that hides a real defect, so raise rather than under-report. An adapter without this method is **declared by name** rather than silently degraded: `core/doctor.py`'s `parity-snapshot` check and `python3 -m core.parity --candidate-adapter <type>` both say which verdicts (`UNRETRIEVABLE`, `ORACLE_MISSED`) are unavailable and why, so a permanently fail-closed run (push surfaces like `slack_socket`; any Telegram bot) reads as a missing capability, not a read-path defect. |
+| `thread_summary(message) -> {reply_count, repliers, latest_reply} \| None` | What one polled row says about the thread under it, read back from the row the adapter itself normalized: Slack serves a thread PARENT on `conversations.history` with `reply_count`, `reply_users` (the repliers, up to the few it names) and `latest_reply`, and the first poll of a channel serves every parent that way. `core/threads.py answered_from_summary()` ties an open ask whose summary names a replier other than the asker, at ingest and with no platform call (`threaded_is_answered`, on by default) — measured on the aws-do-agent integration 2026-10-31, where asks anyone could see answered on the platform read UNANSWERED until the bounded `replies()` sweep reached them. None for a row with no replies on it and for a reply row (the summary belongs to the parent); `repliers` empty when the platform counted replies but named nobody, which ties nothing (a replier the platform did not name is a guess). An adapter without this method is read as **cannot see a thread summary** (it yields None), never as "no thread"; the sweep stays the route for a thread that grows after its parent was polled. The shipped `fake` and `slack` adapters expose it. |
 | `replies(channel_id, thread_ts, oldest?) -> list[message]` | Every reply under one thread newer than `oldest` (exclusive), oldest-first, in the normalized shape below, **never the parent**, all pages or raise. History polling does not serve thread replies on Slack (a parent arrives with a `reply_count` and nothing else), so without this method an in-thread answer never reaches the engine — measured on the aws-do-agent integration 2026-10-12, where every ask the agent had answered in-thread read UNANSWERED. `core/threads.py` sweeps the open asks' threads each scheduler cycle, journals the replies like any other message, and ties an ask to the first reply from someone other than the asker. An adapter without this method is read by the sweep as **cannot see answers** (it yields None), never as "no answers"; the reference scheduler then has no in-thread route to answered for that channel — the write surface's Reply remains the other route wherever the adapter can send. The shipped `fake` and `slack` adapters expose it. |
 
